  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nHigh pressure moves overhead tonight with clear skies & light winds\nsetting up a chilly night for mid-September. Lows in the low to\nmid 40s are expected across much of the area, with upper 30s \npossible in the typical cooler locations of central Minnesota & \nwestern Wisconsin. The combination of light winds, clear skies, \n& lingering moisture from yesterdays rain could also result in \nsome patchy fog overnight, mainly in rural valleys & other low- \nlying areas. Wednesday stays mostly quiet, although a weak \nshortwave moving through southwest flow will likely generate scattered\nshowers and a few thunderstorms across the area during the later\nafternoon & evening. Thursday looks dry & a little warmer as \nwarm advection increases ahead of the next system, with highs \nreturning to the mid to upper 70s.\n\nFriday continues to look like the main period of interest this\nweek as a stronger shortwave moves out of the Plains & a 40 kt \nlow- level jet pulls anomalously high moisture northwards into \nthe region. Rain should spread from southwest to northeast \nduring the morning, becoming widespread by afternoon & \ncontinuing into Friday night before tapering early Saturday. \nPWAT values around or above 2\" should support efficient \nrainfall rates, so confidence is fairly high that much of \nsouthern Minnesota & western Wisconsin sees a soaking rain. \nWhere the heaviest axis sets up remains the big question. Some \ndeterministic guidance still spreads a swath of 1-3\" through \nmuch of the area, while a growing number of ensemble members \nfavor a farther south solution with the greatest amounts closer \nto the Minnesota/Iowa border. The AI guidance, particularly the \nAIFS, has also consistently favored this more southern \nplacement. Thus, confidence is increasing that far- southern \nMinnesota has the best chance for the heaviest rain, potentially\nextending eastwards into southwest Wisconsin, while amounts \ncould drop off more quickly farther north towards central \nMinnesota if this southern trend holds. Latest NBM probabilities\nat least support widespread precipitation along & south of \nInterstate 94, so even locations north of the favored heavy-rain\naxis still have a good shot at measurable rain.\n\nA few stronger thunderstorms cant be ruled out near the surface\nwarm front, but this threat will depend greatly on how far north\nthe warm sector can reach. Current guidance generally keeps the\nfront near the Minnesota/Iowa border, which would keep the\nbetter instability & severe potential near or south of far-\nsouthern Minnesota. Heavy rain therefore remains the more likely\nconcern for our area. Much of the weekend trends drier behind\nthe system, although a weakening upper low approaching from the\nDakotas could bring scattered showers back into the region\nSunday. Temperatures remain near normal through the period with\nhighs generally in the upper 60s to 70s.",
